<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Songhua River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sungari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Songhua is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the largest tributary of the Amur. It rises at Heaven Lake on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-paektu">Changbai Mountain</a> and flows north-west and then north-east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a> to join the Amur on the Russian border. Its fertile plain was home to Buyeo and to later peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songhua-river.jpg" alt="Songhua River" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/GSFC/METI/ERSDAC/JAROS, and the U.S./Japan ASTER Science Team. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SonghuaRiver_ASTER_20020401.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
